INT. MOBILE CLINIC - CONVERTED BOOKMOBILE - DAY

A city bookmobile gutted and refitted. Shelves that once held paperbacks now hold gauze and saline. A folding exam bed. A hand-lettered sign: CLOSE THE DOOR SOFTLY.

DR. HALVORSEN LUNDQVIST, fifties, tired, sits on a stool with a blood pressure cuff in her lap. NURSE PIA OKONKWO, thirties, wipes down a tray. ELIAS BRANDT, seventy, shirtless to the waist, sits on the exam bed. His wife MRS. DOROTA BRANDT, also seventy, stands by the door with her purse held in front of her like a shield.

Elias grips the bed's edge. Dr. Lundqvist and Pia look at the monitor at the same time. Pia reaches for the cuff. The doctor reaches for it too. Their hands meet on it and they both stop.

DR. LUNDQVIST
Go ahead.

Pia wraps the cuff around Elias's arm. Dorota takes a step in. Elias lifts his free hand to stop her.

ELIAS
I'm fine.

DOROTA
You're grey.

ELIAS
It's the light in here.

DR. LUNDQVIST
It's not the light, Mr. Brandt.

Pia pumps the bulb. Everyone watches the needle fall.

PIA
One-sixty over ninety-eight.

DR. LUNDQVIST
That's better than it was twenty minutes ago.

DOROTA
What was it twenty minutes ago?

DR. LUNDQVIST
One-ninety over a hundred and ten.

Dorota sits on the bench seat under the window. She puts her purse on her knees and then, after a moment, on the floor.

DOROTA
He wouldn't come in. For three days, he wouldn't come in.

ELIAS
I had the garden.

DOROTA
You had a chest like a drum and you had the garden.

ELIAS
Dorota.

DOROTA
Don't Dorota me in front of the nurse.

PIA
It's all right, Mrs. Brandt.

DOROTA
It isn't, though. I'm sorry. It isn't.

DR. LUNDQVIST
Mr. Brandt, how long have you had the pain in your left arm?

ELIAS
Since Tuesday.

DOROTA
Since Sunday.

ELIAS
Since Sunday. Fine.

DR. LUNDQVIST
And the shortness of breath?

ELIAS
When I climb the stairs.

DOROTA
When he climbs anything.

PIA
We're going to do an ECG. It's painless. Some stickers on your chest.

ELIAS
I know what an ECG is.

PIA
I know you know.

Pia opens a drawer and peels three electrodes from a sheet. Elias watches her hands.

ELIAS
My brother had one of those.

DR. LUNDQVIST
What happened?

ELIAS
He's dead.

DOROTA
Elias.

ELIAS
You asked.

DR. LUNDQVIST
I did. I'm sorry for the loss. When was this?

ELIAS
Nineteen ninety-one. Heart attack at fifty-four. He was cutting a hedge.

DR. LUNDQVIST
That is useful for me to know. I'm going to write it down.

Dr. Lundqvist writes. Pia presses the stickers to his chest, left, right, low.

PIA
Cold?

ELIAS
Everything's cold.

DOROTA
The heater's broken. They said Thursday.

PIA
They said Thursday last Thursday.

DOROTA
They did.

A small laugh passes through the bookmobile, the first. It dies quickly.

DR. LUNDQVIST
Okay. Lie back for me.

Elias lies back. Pia clips the leads on. The monitor draws a line, then another, then a rhythm.

Dr. Lundqvist and Pia both lean toward the screen. They look at each other. Neither speaks. Dr. Lundqvist exhales through her nose.

DR. LUNDQVIST (CONT'D)
No ST elevation.

PIA
No.

DR. LUNDQVIST
That's good.

DOROTA
What does that mean?

DR. LUNDQVIST
It means he is not having a heart attack right now. It does not mean he did not have a small one on Sunday.

DOROTA
Can you tell?

DR. LUNDQVIST
We can draw blood. It takes about an hour at the hospital.

ELIAS
I'm not going to the hospital.

DOROTA
You are going to the hospital.

ELIAS
Dorota.

DOROTA
You are going to the hospital and I am going to sit in the plastic chair and hold your coat.

ELIAS
Who'll feed the cat?

DOROTA
The cat has eaten since nineteen eighty-eight without you.

ELIAS
That's a lie. I fed it this morning.

DOROTA
That is what I mean.

Elias laughs, once, and winces, and presses a hand flat to his sternum.

DR. LUNDQVIST
Stop laughing. Pia, aspirin, please. Chewed, not swallowed.

PIA
Right away.

DR. LUNDQVIST
And call the ambulance. Tell them stable, chest pain, seventy-year-old male.

Pia takes a phone from the shelf. Dorota rises, goes to the bed, and puts her hand over her husband's hand on his chest. He looks at the ceiling. She looks at the monitor.

DR. LUNDQVIST (CONT'D)
Mrs. Brandt, he'll be fine for the next little while. I'd like you to sit with him.

DOROTA
I am sitting with him.

DR. LUNDQVIST
Yes. Thank you.

PIA
(into phone)
Hello? Yes. Mobile clinic on Ostrow Street, by the bakery.

ELIAS
(to Dorota)
Don't let them touch my garden tools.

DOROTA
Nobody wants your garden tools.

ELIAS
The good trowel.

DOROTA
I will guard the trowel with my life.

Pia hangs up and hands Elias an aspirin. He puts it in his mouth and grimaces. The monitor goes on drawing its line.
